==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|indemnité}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|indemnitas||security from damage}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|indemnis||undamaged}}, from {{m|la|in-||not}} + {{m|la|damnum||damage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪnˈdɛmnɪti/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-indemnity.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|in|dem|ni|ty|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|indemnities}}

# Security from damage, loss, or penalty.
# {{lb|en|legal}} An [[obligation]] or [[duty]] upon an individual to incur the losses of another.
# [[repayment|Repayment]]; [[compensation]] for loss or injury.
#* {{quote-book|year=1859|author={{w|John Russell, 1st Earl Russell}}|title=The Life and Times of {{w|Charles James Fox}}|volume=II|location=London|publisher=Richard Bentley|page=363|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0AE-AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA363|passage=It would have been wise to modify rather than revoke the proclamation. But the Allies went further. Now, for the first time, were heard the words of '''indemnity''' for the past, and security for the future.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} The right of an injured party to shift the loss onto the party responsible for the loss.
# {{lb|en|insurance}} A principle of insurance which provides that when a loss occurs, the insured should be restored to the approximate financial condition occupied before the loss occurred, no better, no worse.

